        "answer": {
          "short": "5541.\n\nAdding 3 gives the lowest common multiple of 6, 7, 8, 9 and 11.",
          "worked": "The additional remainders are also 3 less than their divisors. Therefore $n+3$ must be a common multiple of 6, 7, 8, 9 and 11.\n\nFrom part a, it must be a multiple of 504. Every multiple of 504 is already divisible by 6. List these multiples until one is divisible by 11:\n\n$504,1008,1512,2016,2520,3024,3528,4032,4536,5040,5544$.\n\nThe first divisible by 11 is $5544=11\\times504$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=5544-3\\\\n&=5541\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $5541=7\\times791+4$, $5541=8\\times692+5$, $5541=9\\times615+6$, $5541=6\\times923+3$ and $5541=11\\times503+8$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
